0,Music of China - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Music_of_China,"Main article: Music of Tibet. Music forms an integral part of Tibetan Buddhism.Tibetan folk music includes a cappella lu songs, which are distinctively high in pitch with glottal vibrations, as well as now rare epic bards who sing the tales of Gesar, Tibet's most popular hero.",Seke,Sino-Tibetan
1,Dinglebokto Song - Seke/Tibetan - YouTubeSong for a Daughter Married Off - Seke/Tibetan - YouTubeDatasets – Hugging FaceSeke: Nepal’s Forgotten Tibetan Dialect - yoamore.comDocumentation and Description of Seke (Nepal) / सेके (नेपाल ...Tibetan Music & Audio — Stream & Download Traditional Music ...सेके | सुन्दर पाउ नेपाल,https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZTidGlbi36E,"Apr 17, 2026 · Tashi Gurung sings and explains a song about a place called Dinglebokto, up on Mustang's border with Tibet (and now also between Nepal and China), where he used to go for work when he was young.... Apr 17, 2026 · Seke speaker Tashi Gurung, from Gyakar in Mustang (Nepal), sings a song about a daughter being married off. Apr 23, 2026 · 1 Full-text search Edit filters Sort: Trending Active filters: sino-tibetan Clear all Seke belongs to the Tamangic languages within the wider realm of Sino-Tibetan dialects. It is heard in five villages in Mustang Nepal, near the Tibetan border, where deep contact with Tibetan speech has shaped sound and syntax more than in related tongues like Tamang, Gurung, Thakali, and Chantyal. English: This collection includes recordings in and about the Seke language from the lower part of Upper Mustang in northern Nepal, including the varieties spoken in the town of Tshugsang (Chhusang), Tsangle (Chele), Timi (Tetang), Gyugu (Gyakar), and Tangbe. Stream rare recordings of traditional Tibetan instruments, monastic chanting, and contemporary Tibetan artists. The Seke (Tangbes) are an indigenous people residing in the Barhagaun area of Mustang. They resemble Lhopas in physical appearance and are influenced by Tibetan culture.",Seke,Sino-Tibetan
